# Solution — GIAM 1.6 Problem 1

## Problem

Rational Approximation is a field of mathematics that has received much study. The main idea is to find rational numbers that are very good approximations to given irrationals. For example, $ 22/7 $ is a well-known rational approximation to $ \pi $. Find good rational approximations to $ \sqrt 2 $, $ \sqrt 3 $, $ \sqrt 5 $, and $ e $.

## The Method — Continued Fractions

There is one systematic way to produce the *best possible* rational approximations, and it uses exactly the machinery of Section 1.5. Run the Euclidean algorithm on the number (allowing an irrational input): the successive quotients form a **continued fraction**, and truncating it gives the **convergents** $ p/q $ — the fractions that approximate better than *any* fraction with a smaller denominator. The famous $ 22/7 \approx \pi $ is just the first interesting convergent of $ \pi $.

Below, "$ [a_0; a_1, a_2, \dots] $" denotes $ a_0 + \cfrac{1}{a_1 + \cfrac{1}{a_2 + \cdots}} $, and a bar marks the repeating block.

## The Answers

| number | good approximation | decimal | error |
| :---   | :---:              | :---    | :---  |
| $ \sqrt 2 $ | $ \boxed{\dfrac{577}{408}} $ | $ 1.4142156\ldots $ | $ \approx +2\times 10^{-6} $ |
| $ \sqrt 3 $ | $ \boxed{\dfrac{97}{56}} $   | $ 1.7321429\ldots $ | $ \approx +9\times 10^{-5} $ |
| $ \sqrt 5 $ | $ \boxed{\dfrac{682}{305}} $ | $ 2.2360656\ldots $ | $ \approx -2\times 10^{-6} $ |
| $ e $       | $ \boxed{\dfrac{193}{71}} $  | $ 2.7183099\ldots $ | $ \approx +3\times 10^{-5} $ |

Simpler (slightly less accurate) choices are $ \sqrt2\approx \tfrac{99}{70} $, $ \sqrt3\approx\tfrac{71}{41} $, $ \sqrt5\approx\tfrac{161}{72} $, $ e\approx\tfrac{87}{32} $.

## Working — The Four Continued Fractions

**$ \sqrt 2 = [1; \overline{2}] = [1;2,2,2,\dots] $.** Convergents:

$$$
1,\; \tfrac{3}{2},\; \tfrac{7}{5},\; \tfrac{17}{12},\; \tfrac{41}{29},\; \tfrac{99}{70},\; \tfrac{239}{169},\; \tfrac{577}{408},\ \dots
$$$

**$ \sqrt 3 = [1; \overline{1,2}] = [1;1,2,1,2,\dots] $.** Convergents:

$$$
1,\; 2,\; \tfrac{5}{3},\; \tfrac{7}{4},\; \tfrac{19}{11},\; \tfrac{26}{15},\; \tfrac{71}{41},\; \tfrac{97}{56},\ \dots
$$$

**$ \sqrt 5 = [2; \overline{4}] = [2;4,4,4,\dots] $.** Convergents:

$$$
2,\; \tfrac{9}{4},\; \tfrac{38}{17},\; \tfrac{161}{72},\; \tfrac{682}{305},\ \dots
$$$

**$ e = [2; 1,2,1,1,4,1,1,6,1,1,8,\dots] $** (the pattern $ 1,2k,1 $ repeats; not periodic). Convergents:

$$$
2,\; 3,\; \tfrac{8}{3},\; \tfrac{11}{4},\; \tfrac{19}{7},\; \tfrac{87}{32},\; \tfrac{106}{39},\; \tfrac{193}{71},\ \dots
$$$

## The Convergents at a Glance

!(98%)[Continued fractions and convergents for the four irrationals, with the recommended pick boxed](problem-1-approx.svg "For each of √2, √3, √5, e: its continued-fraction expansion (periodic for the square roots, patterned for e) and the list of convergents with decimal values and errors. Recommended picks: √2 ≈ 577/408 (error 2×10⁻⁶), √3 ≈ 97/56 (9×10⁻⁵), √5 ≈ 682/305 (2×10⁻⁶), e ≈ 193/71 (3×10⁻⁵). The convergents alternate over and under the true value and shrink in error rapidly. Note 22/7 ≈ π is the convergent [3;7] of π.")

## Remark — Why Convergents Are the *Best* Approximations

A convergent $ p/q $ of a number $ x $ is not merely *a* good fraction — it is optimal in a precise sense: no other fraction with denominator $ \le q $ comes closer to $ x $. Moreover it satisfies

$$$
\left| x - \frac{p}{q} \right| < \frac{1}{q^2},
$$$

so the error shrinks *faster* than the denominator grows. That is why $ 577/408 $ pins down $ \sqrt2 $ to six decimal places with a two-digit-ish denominator, while a "random" fraction like $ 1414/1000 $ (bigger denominator!) is far worse. This $ 1/q^2 $ quality is the whole point of the "rational approximation" field the problem alludes to.

## Remark — The Convergents Bracket the Target

Reading down any convergent list, the values alternate: one is *too big*, the next *too small*, closing in on the true value like a shrinking interval. For $ \sqrt2 $: $ 1 < \tfrac{7}{5} < \tfrac{41}{29} < \sqrt2 < \tfrac{99}{70} < \tfrac{17}{12} < \tfrac{3}{2} $. Even convergents approach from below, odd ones from above, and the gap collapses at each step. This bracketing is a free by-product of the continued-fraction machinery and gives, at every stage, both an over- and an under-estimate.

## Remark — Periodic CFs and Quadratic Irrationals

Notice the square roots have *periodic* continued fractions ($ \sqrt2 = [1;\overline2] $, $ \sqrt5 = [2;\overline4] $, $ \sqrt3 = [1;\overline{1,2}] $) while $ e $ does not. This is a theorem of Lagrange: a continued fraction is eventually periodic **iff** the number is a *quadratic irrational* — a root of a quadratic with integer coefficients, exactly what $ \sqrt2,\sqrt3,\sqrt5 $ are. The periodicity is why the square-root approximations are so easy to extend: just repeat the block. The number $ e $, being *transcendental* (not the root of any polynomial), cannot be periodic — yet it still has the beautifully regular pattern $ [2;1,2,1,1,4,1,1,6,\dots] $, which is how Euler first proved $ e $ irrational.

## Remark — A Shortcut for Square Roots (Newton's Method)

Continued fractions are the *optimal* tool, but there is a quick hands-on alternative for the square roots: Newton's iteration $ x \mapsto \tfrac12\!\left(x + \tfrac{N}{x}\right) $, which *doubles* the number of correct digits each step. Starting from $ x = \tfrac{3}{2} $ for $ \sqrt2 $:

$$$
\tfrac{3}{2} \;\longmapsto\; \tfrac12\!\left(\tfrac32 + \tfrac{2}{3/2}\right) = \tfrac{17}{12} \;\longmapsto\; \tfrac12\!\left(\tfrac{17}{12} + \tfrac{2}{17/12}\right) = \tfrac{577}{408}.
$$$

Strikingly, this lands on the very same $ \tfrac{17}{12} $ and $ \tfrac{577}{408} $ that appear among the convergents — Newton's method here leaps directly to every *other* convergent. It is the practical way to get $ \sqrt N $ to many digits by hand, and it explains why $ 577/408 $ is such a natural answer for $ \sqrt2 $.

## Remark — These Approximations Are Genuinely Old and Useful

$ 22/7 $ for $ \pi $ and $ 17/12 $ for $ \sqrt2 $ are ancient — the Babylonians used sexagesimal equivalents of such fractions for $ \sqrt2 $ four thousand years ago, and $ 22/7 $ was known to Archimedes. The point of the exercise, sitting where it does right after the definition of the rationals, is conceptual: the irrationals $ \sqrt2,\sqrt3,\sqrt5,e $ can never be written *exactly* as a fraction (that is what "irrational" means, and $ \sqrt2 $'s irrationality is proved on the very next page), yet they can be *pinned arbitrarily close* by fractions. The rationals are **dense** in the reals — you can get as near as you like — even though no rational ever hits an irrational on the nose.
